course: dictionary
lesson: 6553
title: What is a newt?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A newt is a small amphibian. Like frogs and toads, it lives partly in water and partly on land. Its clues are its long tail, four short legs and damp skin.
explain: «Newts are amphibians with tails» tells you two things: the group a newt belongs to, and that a tail is part of what makes it a newt. Always ask: what does this word point to in the sentence?
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it in. «Frogs, toads, newts and salamanders are amphibians.» If a newt is an amphibian, it should fit with frogs and toads in that list, and it does.
fact: A newt is a small amphibian with a long tail, four short legs and damp skin.
fact: Amphibians, such as frogs, toads, newts and salamanders, live partly in water and partly on land.
fact: «Newts» can regrow lost legs and tails.
fact: Great crested newts are protected by law in the UK, so it is against the law to harm them.
fact: Newts lay eggs in ponds, and fish can eat the eggs and young, so ponds without fish are safer for them.
exercise: «Newts are amphibians with tails.» What is a newt? | *A small amphibian with a long tail and damp skin | A small bird that sings in spring | A reptile with scales and a hard shell | A kind of fish with fins
exercise: «Out of a thousand eggs only a few become adult frogs, because fish, newts, birds, beetles and dragonfly larvae eat many.» According to the sentence, which animal eats frog eggs? | Cow | Hedgehog | *Beetles | Rabbit
exercise: «Great crested newts are protected by law in the UK.» What does protected by law mean? | Allowed to be caught for food | Chased away from ponds | Kept in cages as pets | *Kept safe, because the law stops people from harming them
test: Which body parts can a newt regrow? | Gills and fins | Horns and antlers | Wings and feathers | *Lost legs and tails
test: Where does a newt live? | *Partly in water and partly on land | Only underground | Only in the sea | Only in trees
exam: Which group of animals is a newt in? | *Amphibians | Birds | Mammals | Reptiles
